<ChapterLayout
  title="5.2 — Formation of Oceans and Atmospheres · AST 100"
  description="How a molten young Earth got its air and its seas — volcanic outgassing, a rain that lasted centuries, an ocean of dissolved iron — and how life then poisoned the whole planet with oxygen."
  activeId="5.2" chapter={5} current="/chapter/5/5.2">
  <Hero
    tease="A newborn Earth had no air worth breathing and no ocean at all. Volcanoes exhaled steam and carbon dioxide; the steam fell as rain for centuries and filled the basins. Then, quietly, life invented a way to eat sunlight — and began pumping out a gas so reactive it rusted the seas, poisoned nearly everything alive, and eventually became a fifth of the air you are breathing right now."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="origins">Where the Air and the Sea Came From</h2>
      <p>
        The Earth that emerged from the collisions of <a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a> was not a place you could stand
        on, let alone breathe on. It was a ball of molten rock, still glowing from the impacts that assembled it, and
        whatever thin atmosphere it had been born with — a wisp of hydrogen and helium caught from the disk — was
        stripped away by the young Sun's fierce early wind. Earth had to build its air and its ocean a second time,
        from the inside out.
      </p>
      <p>
        The source was <strong>volcanic outgassing</strong>. Rock, when it melts, releases the gases dissolved in it,
        and the young Earth was one enormous volcano. What came out was mostly <strong>water vapour</strong>, along with
        <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> and <strong>nitrogen</strong> — a hot, thick, heavy atmosphere with essentially
        <em>no oxygen</em> in it at all. This surprises people, but it is worth pausing on: for the first half of Earth's
        history, an unprotected human would have suffocated in seconds. The air we regard as normal is a recent
        invention, and life invented it.
      </p>
      <p>
        As the surface cooled below the boiling point, that atmospheric steam did what steam does — it condensed. The
        rain that followed is thought to have fallen more or less continuously for centuries, and it filled the low
        basins of the crust to make the first <strong>oceans</strong>. We can put a rough date on it from an unlikely
        witness: tiny crystals of the mineral zircon, found in Australia and dated to <strong>4.4 billion years
        ago</strong>, carry a chemical signature that suggests they formed in the presence of liquid water. Earth got
        wet astonishingly early — within a couple of hundred million years of forming.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of that water was delivered rather than exhaled. Water carries a fingerprint — the ratio of ordinary
        hydrogen to its heavier cousin, deuterium — and Earth's ocean fingerprint matches the water locked inside
        <strong>carbonaceous asteroids</strong>, the dark, water-rich bodies from beyond the snow line
        (<a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a>). It does <em>not</em> match most comets: when the Rosetta spacecraft
        measured comet 67P in 2015, its water was three times heavier than ours. So the old story that "comets brought
        the oceans" has largely given way to asteroids, and it is a lovely example of how an isotope ratio can settle
        an argument about the deep past.
      </p>

      <h2 id="oxygen">The Great Poisoning</h2>
      <p>
        For its first two billion years, Earth was a living planet with an unbreathable atmosphere. The microbes of
        <a href={subPath("5.3")}>§5.3</a> lived without oxygen, drawing energy from chemistry — from volcanic gases,
        from minerals, from sunlight in ways that did not release oxygen. Then, at some point deep in the Archean,
        one lineage of microbes — the <strong>cyanobacteria</strong> — hit on a new trick:
        <strong>oxygenic photosynthesis</strong>. Use sunlight to prise hydrogen out of water, keep the hydrogen to
        build sugars, and throw away the leftover oxygen.
      </p>
      <p>
        It was the most consequential act of pollution in the history of the planet. Free oxygen is violently
        reactive — it is precisely why it is useful for burning things, including food — and to the anaerobic life that
        ruled the world, it was a poison. Slide through the whole four-billion-year record of atmospheric oxygen in the
        figure below (the vertical axis is logarithmic, so each step up is ten times more oxygen); the panel underneath
        tells you the air, the ocean, and the life of the moment you are standing in.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Four billion years of atmospheric oxygen">
        <OxygenPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        The curve has a strange shape, and the strangest thing about it is the delay. Cyanobacteria were making oxygen
        for hundreds of millions of years before any of it showed up in the air. Where was it going? Into the ocean —
        specifically, into the <strong>iron</strong> dissolved in it. Without oxygen, iron stays dissolved in seawater;
        the early ocean held a colossal amount of it. Every oxygen molecule released was immediately seized by that
        iron and dropped to the sea floor as rust. The ocean was an oxygen sponge the size of a planet, and it took
        hundreds of millions of years to fill.
      </p>
      <p>
        We can still see the receipt. Those rusted layers, laid down season after season, are the
        <strong>banded iron formations</strong> — the striped red rock that supplies most of the world's iron ore.
        The steel in a bridge is the fossilised exhaust of Earth's first breath. Step through the three states of the
        ocean in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The changing chemistry of the ocean">
        <OceanRedoxPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        When the sponge finally saturated, oxygen flooded into the atmosphere — the <strong>Great Oxidation
        Event</strong>, roughly <strong>2.4 to 2.2 billion years ago</strong>. For the anaerobes it was a catastrophe;
        they were driven into the airless corners of the world where their descendants still live — deep mud, hot
        springs, the inside of your gut. (Microbes leave too poor a fossil record to count the dead, so we speak of this
        as a near-certain inference rather than a documented extinction.) But for what came after, oxygen was a gift.
        Burning food with oxygen yields roughly <strong>ten times</strong> more energy than the airless chemistry that
        preceded it, and that energy budget is what pays for an animal. High in the atmosphere, oxygen also assembled
        itself into <strong>ozone</strong> — a molecule of three oxygen atoms — forming the shield that absorbs the
        Sun's harshest ultraviolet and, in time, made it safe for life to crawl out of the water.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a lesson here that a course in cosmic history has no business skipping. Life is not a passenger on this
        planet. It rebuilt the atmosphere, rusted the oceans, changed the colour of the sky, and drove — as far as we
        can tell — the largest ecological upheaval in Earth's history, entirely as a by-product of making a living.
        The air you are breathing is not a natural feature of a rocky planet. It is exhaust.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>Outgassed steam, a rain of centuries, an ocean of iron, and then the slow, planetary poisoning that made
        the air breathable. But we have been talking about "life" as if it were already here. Where did it come
        from?</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

